{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:17172348 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:17172348 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"17172348","source":"MED","pmid":"17172348","pmcid":"PMC1855727","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC1855727"]},"doi":"10.1128/jb.01192-06","title":"A gene cluster involved in degradation of substituted salicylates via ortho cleavage in Pseudomonas sp. strain MT1 encodes enzymes specifically adapted for transformation of 4-methylcatechol and 3-methylmuconate.","authorString":"Cámara B, Bielecki P, Kaminski F, dos Santos VM, Plumeier I, Nikodem P, Pieper DH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cámara B","lastName":"Cámara","firstName":"Beatriz","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5227-7499"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Microbiology, HZI-Helmholtz Zentrum für Infektionsforschung, Inhoffenstrasse 7, D-38124 Braunschweig, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Bielecki P","lastName":"Bielecki","firstName":"Piotr","initials":"P"},{"fullName":"Kaminski F","lastName":"Kaminski","firstName":"Filip","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"dos Santos VM","lastName":"dos Santos","firstName":"Vitor Martins","initials":"VM"},{"fullName":"Plumeier I","lastName":"Plumeier","firstName":"Iris","initials":"I"},{"fullName":"Nikodem P","lastName":"Nikodem","firstName":"Patricia","initials":"P"},{"fullName":"Pieper DH","lastName":"Pieper","firstName":"Dietmar H","initials":"DH"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5227-7499"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2352-9017"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"189","journalIssueId":1378432,"dateOfPublication":"2007 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2007,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Bacteriol","title":"Journal of bacteriology","essn":"1098-5530","issn":"0021-9193","isoabbreviation":"J Bacteriol","nlmid":"2985120R"},"printPublicationDate":"2007-03-01"},"pubYear":"2007","pageInfo":"1664-1674","abstractText":"Pseudomonas sp. strain MT1 has recently been reported to degrade 4- and 5-chlorosalicylate by a pathway assumed to consist of a patchwork of reactions comprising enzymes of the 3-oxoadipate pathway. Genes encoding the initial steps in the degradation of salicylate and substituted derivatives were now localized and sequenced. One of the gene clusters characterized (sal) showed a novel gene arrangement, with salA, encoding a salicylate 1-hydroxylase, being clustered with salCD genes, encoding muconate cycloisomerase and catechol 1,2-dioxygenase, respectively, and was expressed during growth on salicylate and chlorosalicylate. A second gene cluster (cat), exhibiting the typical catRBCA arrangement of genes of the catechol branch of the 3-oxoadipate pathway in Pseudomonas strains, was expressed during growth on salicylate. Despite their high sequence similarities with isoenzymes encoded by the cat gene cluster, the catechol 1,2-dioxygenase and muconate cycloisomerase encoded by the sal cluster showed unusual kinetic properties. Enzymes were adapted for turnover of 4-chlorocatechol and 3-chloromuconate; however, 4-methylcatechol and 3-methylmuconate were identified as the preferred substrates. Investigation of the substrate spectrum identified 4- and 5-methylsalicylate as growth substrates, which were effectively converted by enzymes of the sal cluster into 4-methylmuconolactone, followed by isomerization to 3-methylmuconolactone. The function of the sal gene cluster is therefore to channel both chlorosubstituted and methylsubstituted salicylates into a catechol ortho cleavage pathway, followed by dismantling of the formed substituted muconolactones through specific pathways.","affiliation":"Division of Microbiology, HZI-Helmholtz Zentrum für Infektionsforschung, Inhoffenstrasse 7, D-38124 Braunschweig, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pseudomonas","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Salicylates","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Catechols","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intramolecular Lyases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IP","qualifierName":"isolation & purification"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mixed Function Oxygenases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IP","qualifierName":"isolation & purification"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sorbic Acid","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AA","qualifierName":"analogs & derivatives"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Reverse Transcriptase Polymerase Chain Reaction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Base Sequence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Multigene Family"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Molecular Sequence Data"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Catechol 1,2-Dioxygenase","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"catechol 1,2-dioxygenase",